@@s001 Code: Kruskal in Java
@@s002 Code: Kruskal in Java
@@s003 The minimum spanning tree in Java: edges sorted by weight, union-find with path compression, and the cycle test.
@@s004 Find with path compression
@@s005 The <code>find</code> function climbs to the root. Each node met is linked directly to the root.
@@s006 Find with path compression
@@s007 Recursive method. The field <code>padre</code> is static: all the calls share it.
@@s008 Path compression: the parent of <code>x</code> becomes the root found. Future calls cost less.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>static</code> on methods and fields: the <code>Main</code> class creates no objects, it only uses shared variables.
@@s011 Kruskal
@@s012 I sort the edges by increasing weight and take those that connect different components.
@@s013 Kruskal
@@s014 Each edge is <code>{u, v, weight}</code>: three columns in a two-dimensional array.
@@s015 I sort by weight, column 2.
@@s016 Different roots: the edge does not close a cycle. I take it.
@@s017 The total cost is a <code>long</code>: sums of many weights can exceed the capacity of an <code>int</code>.
@@s018 Syntax notes
@@s019 <code>long</code> has 64 bits, <code>int</code> 32. The suffix <code>L</code> in literals makes them long: <code>1000000000L</code>.
@@s020 The comment after <code>}</code> is allowed: the code and the comment can be on the same line.
@@s021 Running it
@@s022 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
